---
import BenchmarkrWireframe from "../components/case-study/BenchmarkrWireframe.astro";
import CaseStudyHero from "../components/case-study/CaseStudyHero.astro";
import PullQuote from "../components/case-study/PullQuote.astro";
import StorySection from "../components/case-study/StorySection.astro";
import WireframePlaceholder from "../components/case-study/WireframePlaceholder.astro";
import { Body, Eyebrow } from "../components/typography";
import {
  benchmarkrActivities,
  benchmarkrFacts,
  benchmarkrNextSteps,
  benchmarkrVisuals,
  pilotMeasures,
} from "../data/benchmarkr";
import CaseStudyLayout from "../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";

const [
  heroVisual,
  paperVisual,
  separateProcessesVisual,
  stepBuilderVisual,
  processInstanceVisual,
  verificationVisual,
  needsReviewVisual,
  nextStepsVisual,
] = benchmarkrVisuals;

const meetlyHref = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}meetly-navigation.html`;
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Benchmarkr case study | Jordan England Nelson"
  description="How Jordan England Nelson designed and built a flexible bilingual quality-control product around real shop-floor work for an ongoing manufacturing pilot."
>
  <CaseStudyHero
    eyebrow="Benchmarkr · Product strategy, design, and implementation"
    title="From rigid paper checklists to flexible factory quality control"
    lede="How I designed and built Benchmarkr around the messy reality of shop-floor work, then piloted it on a fire-table SKU at a 60-person concrete furniture manufacturer in Mexico."
    facts={benchmarkrFacts}
    activities={benchmarkrActivities}
  >
    <WireframePlaceholder
      slot="visual"
      index={heroVisual.index}
      title={heroVisual.title}
      description={heroVisual.description}
      caption={heroVisual.caption}
      device={heroVisual.device}
      ratio={heroVisual.ratio}
      status={heroVisual.status}
    >
      <BenchmarkrWireframe scene={heroVisual.scene} />
    </WireframePlaceholder>

    <aside class="case-intro-summary" aria-label="Project problem">
      <Body>
        The manufacturer had outgrown paper checklists and spreadsheets, but complex enterprise
        manufacturing software was a poor fit. Approved standards were difficult to reference at
        the point of work, while the checklists defining each SKU presented a cleaner and more
        rigid process than managers actually followed. Benchmarkr’s ongoing pilot connects the
        standard, each serialized product, inspection evidence, and management decisions in one
        bilingual workflow.
      </Body>
    </aside>
  </CaseStudyHero>

  <StorySection
    id="pilot-changed"
    eyebrow="The hook"
    title="The factory floor changed the pilot before it began"
    layout="prose"
  >
    <PullQuote
      quote="Let’s test this on the guys with clipboards, not the guys with gloves who are covered in concrete."
      attribution="Lead shop-floor manager"
    />
    <Body>
      I heard that sentence while showing the first MVP to the lead shop-floor manager and planning
      the pilot. I expected a debate about whether manufacturing and inspection should be one
      workflow or two. Instead, he exposed a physical constraint that buyer-led discovery had
      missed: fabricators worked in heavy gloves, in an environment covered with concrete, where
      navigating an iPad touchscreen was impractical.
    </Body>
    <Body>
      The first pilot narrowed to inspectors—the people already carrying clipboards. That did not
      mean fabricators saw no value in process guidance; the interaction method was the constraint.
      The flexible process model described later was already in place, so this moment changed who
      the pilot was for rather than causing the model redesign.
    </Body>
    <div class="case-boundary-note">
      <Body>
        That sentence changed who the first pilot was for—and exposed what I had missed by
        beginning with the buyer rather than the people closest to the work.
      </Body>
    </div>
  </StorySection>

  <StorySection
    id="standard-separated"
    eyebrow="The business problem"
    title="The approved standard was separated from the work"
    layout="prose"
    tone="tinted"
  >
    <Body>
      The factory produces heavy concrete furniture for a major U.S. luxury home-furnishings
      retailer. Finish, color, gloss, and overall appearance could take months and dozens of
      refinement rounds before one physical sample was approved.
    </Body>
    <Body>
      That large sample was hard to move and could be far from the active work. People sometimes
      had to rely on memory, while remote leadership lacked an immediate view of each
      unit’s quality status. An unacceptable result might be discovered only after substantial
      labor and material had already been added.
    </Body>
    <div class="case-boundary-note">
      <Body>
        <strong>Intended impact—not a measured result:</strong> connect the approved standard,
        per-unit work, evidence, and review while intervention is still possible. The ongoing pilot
        has not yet produced measured quality or cost outcomes.
      </Body>
    </div>
  </StorySection>

  <StorySection
    id="paper-versus-practice"
    eyebrow="Stated process versus practiced process"
    title="Paper documented the intended process—not the practiced process"
    layout="split"
  >
    <Body>
      Initial discovery centered on the buyer—the factory owner—and the PDF process checklists he
      had created. The documents appeared to define clear, linear SKU steps. They also made
      manufacturing and inspection look more distinct than they were on the floor.
    </Body>
    <Body>
      Conversations with shop-floor managers exposed informal adaptations, exceptions,
      overlapping responsibilities, and inconsistent checklist use. The problem was not simply
      that workers ignored instructions. The checklists were too rigid to represent how the work
      was actually practiced.
    </Body>

    <WireframePlaceholder
      slot="visual"
      index={paperVisual.index}
      title={paperVisual.title}
      description={paperVisual.description}
      caption={paperVisual.caption}
      device={paperVisual.device}
      ratio={paperVisual.ratio}
      status={paperVisual.status}
    >
      <BenchmarkrWireframe scene={paperVisual.scene} />
    </WireframePlaceholder>
  </StorySection>

  <StorySection
    id="wrong-abstraction"
    eyebrow="The wrong turn"
    title="The first MVP encoded the wrong abstraction"
    layout="split"
    visualPosition="start"
  >
    <Body>
      An <strong>abstraction</strong> is the simplified model software uses to organize reality. My
      first model created hard “manufacturing process” and “inspection process” types with
      different functionality. It mirrored the buyer’s terminology and the clean divisions in the
      paper artifacts.
    </Body>
    <Body>
      In practice, making and checking could be separate, alternating, or intermixed. I had turned
      a fuzzy, context-dependent organizational distinction into a rigid software rule. The model
      asked the factory to reshape its work around the software instead of letting the software
      adapt to the work.
    </Body>

    <WireframePlaceholder
      slot="visual"
      index={separateProcessesVisual.index}
      title={separateProcessesVisual.title}
      description={separateProcessesVisual.description}
      caption={separateProcessesVisual.caption}
      device={separateProcessesVisual.device}
      ratio={separateProcessesVisual.ratio}
      status={separateProcessesVisual.status}
    >
      <BenchmarkrWireframe scene={separateProcessesVisual.scene} />
    </WireframePlaceholder>
  </StorySection>

  <StorySection
    id="step-level-flexibility"
    eyebrow="The pivotal product decision"
    title="Flexibility belonged at the step level"
    layout="split"
    tone="tinted"
  >
    <Body>
      I replaced the separate typed builders with one configurable process-template builder.
      Instead of assigning an entire process a type, managers assemble each Step from the
      capabilities it needs: Tasks, guidance, attachments, signatures, and inspection requirements
      on the relevant Tasks.
    </Body>
    <Body>
      The same builder can create manufacturing-only, inspection-only, or mixed workflows. A
      reusable Process remains easy to understand, while the configuration can vary where the
      physical work actually varies.
    </Body>
    <div class="principle-callout">
      <Body>
        <strong>This is composition over classification:</strong> assembling behavior from small
        capabilities instead of forcing an entire workflow into one fixed category.
      </Body>
    </div>

    <WireframePlaceholder
      slot="visual"
      index={stepBuilderVisual.index}
      title={stepBuilderVisual.title}
      description={stepBuilderVisual.description}
      caption={stepBuilderVisual.caption}
      device={stepBuilderVisual.device}
      ratio={stepBuilderVisual.ratio}
      status={stepBuilderVisual.status}
    >
      <BenchmarkrWireframe scene={stepBuilderVisual.scene} />
    </WireframePlaceholder>
  </StorySection>

  <StorySection
    id="pilot-narrowed"
    eyebrow="Architecture meets the factory"
    title="Physical reality narrowed the pilot"
    layout="prose"
  >
    <Body>
      With the flexible model in place, I expected one of two patterns: separate manufacturing and
      inspection workflows, or one alternating workflow assigned by role. Managers chose neither.
      They created an inspector-only quality process because quality control was the highest-priority
      use case and inspectors were already using iPads.
    </Body>
    <Body>
      Touch interaction—not a lack of value—kept gloved fabricators out of the first workflow. The
      architecture survived the scope change because it had stopped treating making and inspecting
      as fixed workflow categories. A barcode-based completion flow at a fixed workstation is a later,
      research-backed direction; it has not been implemented, prototyped, or validated.
    </Body>
  </StorySection>

  <StorySection
    id="product-journey"
    eyebrow="The tested workflow"
    title="One product moved from standard to decision"
    layout="wide"
  >
    <Body>
      The pilot was not a collection of disconnected features. It connected a reusable standard to
      the live history and decisions for one serialized physical product.
    </Body>

    <dl class="journey-definitions">
      <div class="journey-definition">
        <dt>Process template</dt>
        <dd>The reusable blueprint for how a product type should be inspected or made.</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="journey-definition">
        <dt>Process instance</dt>
        <dd>The live run of that blueprint for one physical unit.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <ol class="journey-sequence" aria-label="Benchmarkr pilot journey">
      <li>Reusable process template</li>
      <li>Serialized product + process instance</li>
      <li>Inspector execution</li>
      <li>References and evidence</li>
      <li>Needs Review</li>
      <li>Management decision</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="journey-beats">
      <section class="journey-beat" aria-labelledby="follow-product-title">
        <div class="journey-beat__copy">
          <span class="journey-beat__number">01</span>
          <h3 id="follow-product-title" class="journey-beat__title">Follow the serialized product</h3>
          <Body>
            An inspector opens the pilot fire table and its assigned quality process on an iPad.
            Progress belongs to that individual product rather than to a generic checklist. The
            same process instance carries its current Step, completed Tasks, evidence, and history.
          </Body>
        </div>
        <div class="journey-beat__visual">
          <WireframePlaceholder
            index={processInstanceVisual.index}
            title={processInstanceVisual.title}
            description={processInstanceVisual.description}
            caption={processInstanceVisual.caption}
            device={processInstanceVisual.device}
            ratio={processInstanceVisual.ratio}
            status={processInstanceVisual.status}
          >
            <BenchmarkrWireframe scene={processInstanceVisual.scene} />
          </WireframePlaceholder>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="journey-beat" aria-labelledby="visual-standard-title">
        <div class="journey-beat__copy">
          <span class="journey-beat__number">02</span>
          <h3 id="visual-standard-title" class="journey-beat__title">
            Put the visual standard at the checkpoint
          </h3>
          <Body>
            Approved references and known-defect examples appear on the relevant Task. The
            inspector captures evidence of the actual unit beside them. Benchmarkr organizes the
            comparison; the human inspector—not the software—makes the quality judgment.
          </Body>
        </div>
        <div class="journey-beat__visual">
          <WireframePlaceholder
            index={verificationVisual.index}
            title={verificationVisual.title}
            description={verificationVisual.description}
            caption={verificationVisual.caption}
            device={verificationVisual.device}
            ratio={verificationVisual.ratio}
            status={verificationVisual.status}
          >
            <BenchmarkrWireframe scene={verificationVisual.scene} />
          </WireframePlaceholder>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="journey-beat" aria-labelledby="evidence-decision-title">
        <div class="journey-beat__copy">
          <span class="journey-beat__number">03</span>
          <h3 id="evidence-decision-title" class="journey-beat__title">
            Turn evidence into a decision
          </h3>
          <Body>
            A reported issue routes the product into Needs Review. Leadership can filter the live
            queue by process and inspect step-level issue context to see where work is accumulating.
            A reviewer then chooses Clear review, Send back, or Reject.
          </Body>
          <Body>
            This provides operational visibility into active work. It is not an aggregate analytics
            dashboard, and the queue does not claim a current Step filter.
          </Body>
        </div>
        <div class="journey-beat__visual">
          <WireframePlaceholder
            index={needsReviewVisual.index}
            title={needsReviewVisual.title}
            description={needsReviewVisual.description}
            caption={needsReviewVisual.caption}
            device={needsReviewVisual.device}
            ratio={needsReviewVisual.ratio}
            status={needsReviewVisual.status}
          >
            <BenchmarkrWireframe scene={needsReviewVisual.scene} />
          </WireframePlaceholder>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </StorySection>

  <StorySection
    id="working-software"
    eyebrow="A compact implementation loop"
    title="From product decisions to working software"
    layout="wide"
    tone="dark"
  >
    <div class="implementation-aside">
      <div class="implementation-aside__copy">
        <Body>
          I was Benchmarkr’s only product and design person, and I built the working product myself.
          I documented behavior in product specs and decision records, broke implementation into
          small, verifiable slices, and used an AI-assisted development workflow to accelerate
          execution. I remained responsible for product judgment, implementation review, testing,
          and user validation.
        </Body>
        <Body>
          English and Spanish support was part of the product from the beginning, so the same
          workflow could be used by leadership and shop-floor participants without treating
          language as a later add-on.
        </Body>
      </div>

      <figure class="implementation-diagram">
        <figcaption class="visually-hidden">Spec-driven development and feedback loop</figcaption>
        <ol class="development-loop">
          <li>Factory feedback</li>
          <li>Specs and decision records</li>
          <li>Testable implementation slices</li>
          <li>Code and tests</li>
          <li>Pilot feedback</li>
          <li>Revised decisions</li>
        </ol>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </StorySection>

  <StorySection
    id="ongoing-pilot"
    eyebrow="Evidence and measurement"
    title="An ongoing pilot—not a finished success story"
    layout="wide"
  >
    <Body>
      Measurable quality, time, and cost outcomes are not available yet. The current evidence is a
      working bilingual product tested with factory leadership and floor users—not a finished
      launch, a factory-wide rollout, or proof of business impact.
    </Body>

    <div class="evidence-measures">
      <section class="evidence-panel" aria-labelledby="current-evidence-title">
        <Eyebrow as="p" class="evidence-panel__label">Current evidence</Eyebrow>
        <p id="current-evidence-title" class="evidence-panel__title">What exists today</p>
        <ul class="current-evidence-list">
          <li>A working product in English and Spanish.</li>
          <li>Testing with factory leadership and floor users.</li>
          <li>An inspector-led iPad workflow for one fire-table SKU within the wider pilot.</li>
          <li>A configurable builder with capabilities composed at the Step and Task level.</li>
          <li>Operational visibility through Needs Review and step-level issue context.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="evidence-panel evidence-panel--future" aria-labelledby="future-measures-title">
        <Eyebrow as="p" class="evidence-panel__label">Future pilot measures</Eyebrow>
        <p id="future-measures-title" class="evidence-panel__title">What the pilot still needs to prove</p>
        <ol class="measure-list">
          {pilotMeasures.map((measure) => <li>{measure}</li>)}
        </ol>
      </section>
    </div>

    <div class="current-future-clarifier">
      <Body>
        <strong>Current:</strong> filter the live queue by process and inspect step-level context.
      </Body>
      <Body>
        <strong>Future:</strong> aggregate issue counts and trends by process and Step, once broader
        usage produces enough data to make those patterns meaningful.
      </Body>
    </div>

    <aside class="ethics-note" aria-label="Product ethics and boundaries">
      <span class="ethics-note__mark" aria-hidden="true">H</span>
      <p>
        Benchmarkr supports human judgment; it does not automate defect detection or guarantee
        quality. Photos and signatures are evidence and forcing functions—deliberate pauses that
        make important work harder to skip—not worker surveillance. Traceability supports shared
        accountability and process improvement; it does not automatically assign individual blame.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </StorySection>

  <StorySection
    id="reflection-next"
    eyebrow="Reflection and next steps"
    title="What I would do differently—and what comes next"
    layout="split"
    visualPosition="end"
    tone="tinted"
  >
    <Body>
      I would involve shop-floor managers during initial discovery rather than beginning only with
      the buyer. The buyer supplied the business problem and the formal process. Practitioners
      exposed the exceptions, role overlap, and physical constraints. The lesson is not simply to
      “talk to more users”; buyers and practitioners hold different kinds of truth.
    </Body>

    <div class="reflection-synthesis">
      <Body>
        <strong>The most important product decision</strong> was moving flexibility to the level
        where the work actually varies: the Step. <strong>The most important research lesson</strong>{" "}
        was involving the people closest to that work sooner.
      </Body>
    </div>

    <Eyebrow as="p">Planned after the pilot</Eyebrow>
    <ol class="planned-list">
      {benchmarkrNextSteps.map((nextStep) => <li>{nextStep}</li>)}
    </ol>

    <Body>
      Benchmarkr is still early, but the pilot has already reinforced a central design principle:
      flexibility does not mean adding options everywhere. It means placing decisions at the level
      where real work varies.
    </Body>

    <WireframePlaceholder
      slot="visual"
      index={nextStepsVisual.index}
      title={nextStepsVisual.title}
      description={nextStepsVisual.description}
      caption={nextStepsVisual.caption}
      device={nextStepsVisual.device}
      ratio={nextStepsVisual.ratio}
      status={nextStepsVisual.status}
    >
      <BenchmarkrWireframe scene={nextStepsVisual.scene} />
    </WireframePlaceholder>
  </StorySection>

  <nav class="next-project" aria-label="Next case study">
    <p>Next up</p>
    <a href={meetlyHref}>
      How iterative design helped users find their meeting notes twice as fast{" "}
      <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    </a>
  </nav>
</CaseStudyLayout>
